+{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"b01fc717-b99f-4af7-bbed-bdf7c9c85e02","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture of the project. Let me create tasks to track progress and then implement each service. Let me plan out all 8 services:\n\n1. **Catalog Service** - manages products, emits `PRODUCT_CREATED` event\n2. **Inventory Service** - tracks stock, reserves/releases, emits `LOW_STOCK_ALERT`\n3. **Cart Service** - manages carts, emits `CART_UPDATED` event\n4. **Pricing Service** - calculates totals, listens for `PRODUCT_CREATED` to track prices\n5. **Orders Service** - creates/manages orders, emits `ORDER_CREATED` and `ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED`\n6. **Payment Service** - processes payments with simulation options, emits `PAYMENT_PROCESSED`\n7. **Notifications Service** - listens for order status changes and payment events, records notifications\n8. **Analytics Service** - listens for all events, tracks metrics\n\nLet me implement each one carefully, making sure TypeScript compiles cleanly and all the interfaces are satisfied.\n\nKey things to track:\n- Services must not import from one another\n- All communication is through EventBus events\n- Event names from `Events` constant in types.ts\n- Each factory is `create<Name>Service(bus: EventBus)`\n- Payment additionally accepts optional options parameter\n\nLet me create all the services now.\n\nFor PricingService, it needs to listen for `PRODUCT_CREATED` events to track prices. That means it subscribes to the bus in the factory function.\n\nFor NotificationsService, it needs to listen for `ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED` and `PAYMENT_PROCESSED` events. It also needs to track which orders belong to which users (via `ORDER_CREATED` event).\n\nFor AnalyticsService, it needs to listen for all kinds of events to track metrics.\n\nLet me think about the OrderStatus state machine. The spec says: \"Orders start in a pending state and progress through a status lifecycle (confirmed, shipped, delivered) with the option to cancel.\" So the valid transitions would be:\n- PENDING -> CONFIRMED, CANCELLED\n- CONFIRMED -> SHIPPED, CANCELLED\n- SHIPPED -> DELIVERED, CANCELLED\n- DELIVERED -> (no transitions)\n- CANCELLED -> (no transitions)\n\nActually, the `updateStatus` method just takes an orderId and a status. It doesn't explicitly say to validate transitions, but it says \"Orders start in a pending state and progress through a status lifecycle\". Let me keep it simple - just update the status and emit the event.\n\nWait, the OrderStatusChangedEvent includes `previousStatus`, so when updating, I need to emit the previous status.\n\nLet me think about the Cart events:\n- `addItem`: Should consolidate items for the same product (update quantity instead of adding duplicate)\n- `emit` CART_UPDATED event after changes\n\nFor Pricing:\n- Listens to PRODUCT_CREATED to build a price map\n- calculateTotal takes items with prices already, but also needs to get price from its own tracking\n- Wait, the `calculateTotal` method receives items that include price. But `getPrice` gets price from its internal store that's populated by PRODUCT_CREATED events.\n\nActually, looking at the interface:\n```ts\ncalculateTotal(items: Array<{ productId: string; quantity: number; price: number }>, discountCode?: string): { subtotal: number; discount: number; tax: number; total: number };\ngetPrice(productId: string): number;\n```\n\nSo `calculateTotal` receives items with prices already embedded. But it should probably look up the prices from its own store too? Wait, no - the items come with price, so calculateTotal just uses those. The `getPrice` is separate and looks up from its internal store.\n\nFor PaymentService options:\n```ts\nexport interface PaymentService {\n  processPayment(orderId: string, amount: number): Promise<{ status: PaymentStatus; transactionId?: string; error?: string }>;\n  refund(transactionId: string): Promise<boolean>;\n}\n```\n\nIt needs an optional options parameter in the factory:\n```ts\ncreatePaymentService(bus: EventBus, options?: { mode: 'success' | 'fail' | 'timeout'; delay?: number })\n```\n\nActually wait, the spec says \"Support configurable simulation of success, failure, and timeout scenarios via an options parameter.\" So the factory accepts options.\n\nLet me now implement all services. I'll create tasks and then implement them.\n\nFor uuid generation, the package already has `uuid` as a dependency, so I can use `uuid/v4` or `crypto.randomUUID()` since the target is ES2022.\n\nActually, since we're using Node16 module resolution and ES2022 target, `crypto.randomUUID()` should be available. But to be safe, let me use the uuid package since it's already a dependency.\n\nWait, actually `import { v4 } from 'uuid'` should work. Let me use that.\n\nLet me now write all the service implementations.\n\n### Catalog Service\n```ts\nimport { v4 as uuid } from 'uuid';\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { CatalogService, Events, ProductCreatedEvent } from '../types.js';\n\ninterface Product {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  price: number;\n  category: string;\n}\n\nexport function createCatalogService(bus: EventBus): CatalogService {\n  const products = new Map<string, Product>();\n\n  return {\n    createProduct(name: string, price: number, category: string): string {\n      const id = uuid();\n      products.set(id, { id, name, price, category });\n      const payload: ProductCreatedEvent = { productId: id, name, price, category };\n      bus.emit(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, payload);\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    getProduct(productId: string): Product | null {\n      return products.get(productId) ?? null;\n    },\n\n    listProducts(category?: string): Product[] {\n      const all = Array.from(products.values());\n      if (category) {\n        return all.filter(p => p.category === category);\n      }\n      return all;\n    },\n\n    searchProducts(query: string): Product[] {\n      const lower = query.toLowerCase();\n      return Array.from(products.values()).filter(p =>\n        p.name.toLowerCase().includes(lower) || p.category.toLowerCase().includes(lower)\n      );\n    },\n\n    updateProduct(productId: string, updates: Partial<{ name: string; price: number; category: string }>): void {\n      const product = products.get(productId);\n      if (product) {\n        Object.assign(product, updates);\n      }\n    },\n\n    removeProduct(productId: string): boolean {\n      return products.delete(productId);\n    }\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Inventory Service\n```ts\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { InventoryService, Events, LowStockAlertEvent } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createInventoryService(bus: EventBus): InventoryService {\n  const stock = new Map<string, number>();\n  const thresholds = new Map<string, number>();\n  const reserved = new Map<string, number>(); // tracks reservations per product\n\n  function checkLowStock(productId: string): void {\n    const qty = stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n    const threshold = thresholds.get(productId) ?? 0;\n    if (threshold > 0 && qty <= threshold) {\n      const payload: LowStockAlertEvent = { productId, currentQuantity: qty, threshold };\n      bus.emit(Events.LOW_STOCK_ALERT, payload);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return {\n    setStock(productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      stock.set(productId, quantity);\n      if (!reserved.has(productId)) {\n        reserved.set(productId, 0);\n      }\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n    },\n\n    getStock(productId: string): number {\n      const total = stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n      const res = reserved.get(productId) ?? 0;\n      return total - res;\n    },\n\n    reserve(productId: string, quantity: number): boolean {\n      const available = this.getStock(productId);\n      if (available < quantity) {\n        return false;\n      }\n      reserved.set(productId, (reserved.get(productId) ?? 0) + quantity);\n      return true;\n    },\n\n    release(productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      const current = reserved.get(productId) ?? 0;\n      reserved.set(productId, Math.max(0, current - quantity));\n    },\n\n    setLowStockThreshold(productId: string, threshold: number): void {\n      thresholds.set(productId, threshold);\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n    }\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, `setStock` calls `this.getStock` but `getStock` uses reserved... but `setStock` should set the total stock, not the available. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, the stock map should store total stock. Reserved map stores how much is reserved. `getStock` returns total minus reserved (available stock). `setStock` sets the total.\n\nLet me fix:\n\n```ts\nsetStock(productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n  stock.set(productId, quantity);\n  if (!reserved.has(productId)) {\n    reserved.set(productId, 0);\n  }\n  checkLowStock(productId);\n},\n```\n\nWait, but `setStock` calls `checkLowStock` which reads `stock.get(productId)` (the total) and compares to threshold. That seems right - low stock is about total stock being below threshold, not available stock.\n\nHmm, but what if all 10 items are reserved and total stock is 10? Then available is 0, and the low stock alert on total (10 <= 10) might be debatable. Let me check if low stock is based on total or available. The spec says \"Detect low-stock conditions and alert when stock falls below configurable thresholds.\" I think it means total stock, since that's what you'd replenish. Let me keep it as checking total stock.\n\nActually, the `getStock` method returns available stock (total - reserved). But `checkLowStock` should probably check total stock. Let me just make it consistent.\n\nHmm, wait - if I setStock to some value, the total is that value regardless of reservations. But if I already had 10 items and reserved 5, then setStock(3), the total is now 3 but reserved is still 5, so available would be -2 which is weird. Let me think about this...\n\nActually, `setStock` probably sets the absolute total stock count. If we reserved items before, we should probably still honor those reservations. The available stock could go negative temporarily, but that's an edge case in the simulation.\n\nLet me keep it simple.\n\n### Cart Service\n\n```ts\nimport { v4 as uuid } from 'uuid';\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { CartService, Events, CartUpdatedEvent } from '../types.js';\n\ninterface CartItem {\n  productId: string;\n  quantity: number;\n}\n\ninterface Cart {\n  id: string;\n  userId: string;\n  items: CartItem[];\n}\n\nexport function createCartService(bus: EventBus): CartService {\n  const carts = new Map<string, Cart>();\n\n  function emitCartUpdated(cart: Cart): void {\n    const payload: CartUpdatedEvent = {\n      cartId: cart.id,\n      userId: cart.userId,\n      items: cart.items.map(i => ({ productId: i.productId, quantity: i.quantity }))\n    };\n    bus.emit(Events.CART_UPDATED, payload);\n  }\n\n  return {\n    createCart(userId: string): string {\n      const id = uuid();\n      carts.set(id, { id, userId, items: [] });\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    addItem(cartId: string, productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      const existing = cart.items.find(i => i.productId === productId);\n      if (existing) {\n        existing.quantity += quantity;\n      } else {\n        cart.items.push({ productId, quantity });\n      }\n      emitCartUpdated(cart);\n    },\n\n    removeItem(cartId: string, productId: string): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      cart.items = cart.items.filter(i => i.productId !== productId);\n      emitCartUpdated(cart);\n    },\n\n    getCart(cartId: string): Cart | null {\n      return carts.get(cartId) ?? null;\n    },\n\n    clearCart(cartId: string): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      cart.items = [];\n      emitCartUpdated(cart);\n    },\n\n    updateQuantity(cartId: string, productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      const existing = cart.items.find(i => i.productId === productId);\n      if (existing) {\n        existing.quantity = quantity;\n        emitCartUpdated(cart);\n      }\n    }\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Pricing Service\n\n```ts\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { PricingService, Events, ProductCreatedEvent } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createPricingService(bus: EventBus): PricingService {\n  const prices = new Map<string, number>();\n  const discounts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  bus.on(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as ProductCreatedEvent;\n    prices.set(event.productId, event.price);\n  });\n\n  return {\n    calculateTotal(items: Array<{ productId: string; quantity: number; price: number }>, discountCode?: string): { subtotal: number; discount: number; tax: number; total: number } {\n      const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity, 0);\n      let discount = 0;\n      if (discountCode) {\n        const percentage = discounts.get(discountCode);\n        if (percentage !== undefined) {\n          discount = Math.round(subtotal * percentage) / 100;\n        }\n      }\n      const afterDiscount = subtotal - discount;\n      const tax = Math.round(afterDiscount * 0.1 * 100) / 100;\n      const total = Math.round((afterDiscount + tax) * 100) / 100;\n      return { subtotal, discount, tax, total };\n    },\n\n    addDiscountCode(code: string, percentage: number): void {\n      discounts.set(code, percentage);\n    },\n\n    getPrice(productId: string): number {\n      return prices.get(productId) ?? 0;\n    }\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Orders Service\n\n```ts\nimport { v4 as uuid } from 'uuid';\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { OrderService, OrderStatus, Events, OrderCreatedEvent, OrderStatusChangedEvent } from '../types.js';\n\ninterface Order {\n  id: string;\n  userId: string;\n  status: OrderStatus;\n  total: number;\n}\n\nexport function createOrdersService(bus: EventBus): OrderService {\n  const orders = new Map<string, Order>();\n\n  return {\n    createOrder(userId: string, cartId: string, total: number): string {\n      const id = uuid();\n      orders.set(id, { id, userId, status: OrderStatus.PENDING, total });\n      const createdPayload: OrderCreatedEvent = { orderId: id, userId, cartId, total };\n      bus.emit(Events.ORDER_CREATED, createdPayload);\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    getOrder(orderId: string): Order | null {\n      const order = orders.get(orderId);\n      if (!order) return null;\n      return { ...order };\n    },\n\n    updateStatus(orderId: string, status: OrderStatus): void {\n      const order = orders.get(orderId);\n      if (!order) return;\n      const previousStatus = order.status;\n      order.status = status;\n      const payload: OrderStatusChangedEvent = { orderId, status, previousStatus };\n      bus.emit(Events.ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED, payload);\n    },\n\n    listOrders(userId: string): Array<{ id: string; status: OrderStatus; total: number }> {\n      return Array.from(orders.values())\n        .filter(o => o.userId === userId)\n        .map(o => ({ id: o.id, status: o.status, total: o.total }));\n    }\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Payment Service\n\n```ts\nimport { v4 as uuid } from 'uuid';\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { PaymentService, PaymentStatus, Events, PaymentProcessedEvent } from '../types.js';\n\nexport interface PaymentOptions {\n  mode?: 'success' | 'fail' | 'timeout';\n  delay?: number;\n}\n\nexport function createPaymentService(bus: EventBus, options?: PaymentOptions): PaymentService {\n  const transactions = new Map<string, string>(); // transactionId -> orderId\n  const refunds = new Map<string, boolean>(); // transactionId -> refunded\n\n  return {\n    async processPayment(orderId: string, amount: number): Promise<{ status: PaymentStatus; transactionId?: string; error?: string }> {\n      const delay = options?.delay ?? 100;\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay));\n\n      const mode = options?.mode ?? 'success';\n      let status: PaymentStatus;\n      let transactionId: string | undefined;\n      let error: string | undefined;\n\n      switch (mode) {\n        case 'success':\n          status = PaymentStatus.SUCCESS;\n          transactionId = uuid();\n          transactions.set(transactionId, orderId);\n          refunds.set(transactionId, false);\n          break;\n        case 'fail':\n          status = PaymentStatus.FAILED;\n          error = 'Payment declined';\n          break;\n        case 'timeout':\n          status = PaymentStatus.TIMEOUT;\n          error = 'Payment timed out';\n          break;\n        default:\n          status = PaymentStatus.SUCCESS;\n          transactionId = uuid();\n          transactions.set(transactionId, orderId);\n          refunds.set(transactionId, false);\n      }\n\n      const payload: PaymentProcessedEvent = { orderId, status, transactionId, error };\n      bus.emit(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, payload);\n\n      return { status, transactionId, error };\n    },\n\n    async refund(transactionId: string): Promise<boolean> {\n      if (!transactions.has(transactionId)) return false;\n      refunds.set(transactionId, true);\n      return true;\n    }\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### Notifications Service\n\n```ts\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { NotificationService, Events, OrderStatusChangedEvent, PaymentProcessedEvent, OrderCreatedEvent, NotificationSentEvent } from '../types.js';\n\ninterface Notification {\n  type: string;\n  message: string;\n  timestamp: number;\n}\n\nexport function createNotificationService(bus: EventBus): NotificationService {\n  const subscribers = new Set<string>();\n  const notifications = new Map<string, Notification[]>();\n  const orderUsers = new Map<string, string>(); // orderId -> userId\n\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as OrderCreatedEvent;\n    orderUsers.set(event.orderId, event.userId);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as OrderStatusChangedEvent;\n    const userId = orderUsers.get(event.orderId);\n    if (!userId || !subscribers.has(userId)) return;\n    \n    const message = `Order ${event.orderId} status changed from ${event.previousStatus} to ${event.status}`;\n    if (!notifications.has(userId)) notifications.set(userId, []);\n    const notification: Notification = { type: 'order_status', message, timestamp: Date.now() };\n    notifications.get(userId)!.push(notification);\n    \n    bus.emit(Events.NOTIFICATION_SENT, { userId, type: 'order_status', message } satisfies NotificationSentEvent);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as PaymentProcessedEvent;\n    // Find the userId from the order\n    const userId = getUserIdForOrder(event.orderId);\n    if (!userId || !subscribers.has(userId)) return;\n    \n    let message: string;\n    if (event.status === 'success') {\n      message = `Payment of ${event.transactionId} processed successfully for order ${event.orderId}`;\n    } else {\n      message = `Payment for order ${event.orderId} failed: ${event.error ?? 'unknown error'}`;\n    }\n    \n    if (!notifications.has(userId)) notifications.set(userId, []);\n    const notification: Notification = { type: 'payment', message, timestamp: Date.now() };\n    notifications.get(userId)!.push(notification);\n    \n    bus.emit(Events.NOTIFICATION_SENT, { userId, type: 'payment', message } satisfies NotificationSentEvent);\n  });\n\n  // We need to find userId for a given orderId\n  // Since we track orderUser from ORDER_CREATED, this should work\n  function getUserIdForOrder(orderId: string): string | undefined {\n    return orderUsers.get(orderId);\n  }\n\n  return {\n    subscribe(userId: string): void {\n      subscribers.add(userId);\n      if (!notifications.has(userId)) {\n        notifications.set(userId, []);\n      }\n    },\n\n    getNotifications(userId: string): Notification[] {\n      return notifications.get(userId) ?? [];\n    }\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, there's an issue - the `getUserIdForOrder` function accesses `orderUsers`, but that's in the same scope so it's fine. But I need to be careful about the PAYMENT_PROCESSED event - the notification service needs to know which user the order belongs to. Since we track `orderUsers` from ORDER_CREATED events, this should work IF the order was created before the payment was processed.\n\nBut wait, the notification handler is registered inside the factory function. The `bus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, ...)` handler stores the userId for each orderId. Then when PAYMENT_PROCESSED fires, we look up the userId. Since events are emitted synchronously (well, the bus emits them in order using await), the ORDER_CREATED event should have been processed before PAYMENT_PROCESSED.\n\nActually, looking at the bus.emit implementation:\n```ts\nasync emit(event: string, payload: unknown): Promise<void> {\n  this.eventLog.push({ event, payload, timestamp: Date.now() });\n  const handlers = this.handlers.get(event);\n  if (handlers) {\n    for (const handler of handlers) {\n      await handler(payload);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIt awaits each handler. So if an external caller does:\n1. bus.emit(Events.ORDER_CREATED, ...) → notification handler stores userId → completes\n2. bus.emit(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, ...) → notification handler reads userId → works\n\nSo the ordering is correct as long as these are emitted in sequence. Good.\n\nBut there's another issue - the notification service's handler for ORDER_CREATED uses `orderUsers.set(event.orderId, event.userId)`. The order service creates the order and emits ORDER_CREATED. Then the notification handler stores the mapping. So when ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED or PAYMENT_PROCESSED fires later, the mapping exists. This should work.\n\nBut wait - the PAYMENT_PROCESSED handler accesses `orderUsers.get(event.orderId)`. The `getUserIdForOrder` function is defined in the closure scope and can access `orderUsers`. So that's fine.\n\nActually, I realize there might be a circular issue with the NOTIFICATION_SENT event. When the notification service generates a notification, it emits NOTIFICATION_SENT. But if the Analytics service is also listening for NOTIFICATION_SENT, it would count it. That should be fine - analytics tracks all events.\n\n### Analytics Service\n\nThis needs to listen to events on the bus and track:\n- Event counts by type\n- Revenue from successful payments\n- Conversion rate (orders / unique carts)\n- Top products by order quantity\n\n```ts\nimport { EventBus } from '../bus.js';\nimport { AnalyticsService, Events, PaymentProcessedEvent, OrderCreatedEvent, CartUpdatedEvent } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createAnalyticsService(bus: EventBus): AnalyticsService {\n  const eventCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  let revenue = 0;\n  const uniqueCartIds = new Set<string>();\n  let orderCount = 0;\n  const productOrderCounts = new Map<string, number>(); // productId -> total ordered quantity\n\n  // Track all events\n  const allEvents = Object.values(Events);\n  for (const eventName of allEvents) {\n    bus.on(eventName, (payload: unknown) => {\n      eventCounts.set(eventName, (eventCounts.get(eventName) ?? 0) + 1);\n    });\n  }\n\n  // Track revenue from successful payments\n  bus.on(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as PaymentProcessedEvent;\n    if (event.status === 'success') {\n      // We don't get the amount from the payment event directly...\n      // The PaymentProcessedEvent doesn't include amount\n    }\n  });\n\n  // Track unique carts\n  bus.on(Events.CART_UPDATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as CartUpdatedEvent;\n    uniqueCartIds.add(event.cartId);\n  });\n\n  // Track orders and products\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as OrderCreatedEvent;\n    orderCount++;\n    revenue += event.total; // The OrderCreatedEvent has total!\n  });\n\n  // Track products from cart events\n  bus.on(Events.CART_UPDATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as CartUpdatedEvent;\n    for (const item of event.items) {\n      productOrderCounts.set(item.productId, (productOrderCounts.get(item.productId) ?? 0) + item.quantity);\n    }\n  });\n\n  return {\n    getEventCount(eventType: string): number {\n      return eventCounts.get(eventType) ?? 0;\n    },\n\n    getRevenue(): number {\n      return revenue;\n    },\n\n    getConversionRate(): number {\n      if (uniqueCartIds.size === 0) return 0;\n      return orderCount / uniqueCartIds.size;\n    },\n\n    getTopProducts(limit: number): Array<{ productId: string; count: number }> {\n      return Array.from(productOrderCounts.entries())\n        .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])\n        .slice(0, limit)\n        .map(([productId, count]) => ({ productId, count }));\n    }\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the revenue tracking. The `OrderCreatedEvent` has a `total` field, so I can use that. But which event is more appropriate for revenue? I think order creation is fine since the total is set at that point.\n\nActually, for more accurate revenue tracking, I should probably use successful payments. But the PaymentProcessedEvent doesn't include the amount. Let me reconsider...\n\nLooking at the interface:\n```ts\nexport interface PaymentProcessedEvent { orderId: string; status: PaymentStatus; transactionId?: string; error?: string; }\n```\n\nIt doesn't include amount. So I'll need to get the amount from the OrderCreatedEvent. But by the time the payment is processed, I might not have the order total handy... unless I store it.\n\nLet me store order totals when ORDER_CREATED fires:\n```ts\nconst orderTotals = new Map<string, number>();\n\nbus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n  const event = payload as OrderCreatedEvent;\n  orderTotals.set(event.orderId, event.total);\n  orderCount++;\n});\n\nbus.on(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, (payload: unknown) => {\n  const event = payload as PaymentProcessedEvent;\n  if (event.status === 'success') {\n    revenue += orderTotals.get(event.orderId) ?? 0;\n  }\n});\n```\n\nActually, the spec says \"track revenue from successful payments\". Let me use the PAYMENT_PROCESSED event and look up the order total. This makes more sense.\n\nAlso, for conversion rate: \"orders divided by unique carts\". The order count from ORDER_CREATED divided by unique cart IDs from CART_UPDATED.\n\nFor top products: \"rank products by order quantity\". I should track product quantities from CART_UPDATED events.\n\nActually wait - the CART_UPDATED event fires when the cart is modified. If I count items from CART_UPDATED, I might overcount if items are removed and re-added. But the spec doesn't specify the exact mechanism - let me keep it simple and count from ORDER_CREATED events.\n\nHmm, but OrderCreatedEvent doesn't have items, only total. Let me check:\n```ts\nexport interface OrderCreatedEvent { orderId: string; userId: string; cartId: string; total: number; }\n```\n\nNo items. So I can't get product-level data from ORDER_CREATED. I need to get it from CART_UPDATED or somewhere else.\n\nLooking at the spec more carefully: \"rank products by order quantity\". This likely means the quantity of products that appear in orders. Since we don't have items in the order event, maybe we need to track from CART_UPDATED events, or perhaps from some other mechanism.\n\nActually, I think the most natural thing is to track from CART_UPDATED events - when a cart is updated (presumably as part of creating an order), the items in the cart re