nce `catalog.createProduct` calls `bus.emit()` without `await`, the handlers may not have executed by the time `expect(events).toHaveLength(1)` runs.\n\nHowever, the test for `Catalog: CRUD and search` passes (test 1), because it doesn't check events. And the test for `Catalog: emits PRODUCT_CREATED event` fails because the handler hasn't run yet.\n\nThe fix: I need to make `createProduct` (and other methods that emit events) async. But wait - the interface defines them as synchronous:\n```ts\ncreateProduct(name: string, price: number, category: string): string;\n```\n\nSo I can't change the return type. But in TypeScript, an async function that returns `string` can return `Promise<string>`. The interface says `string` but the implementation could return `Promise<string>` and it would be assignable... actually no, `string` is not assignable from `Promise<string>`. You can't return a promise where a plain value is expected.\n\nBut wait, TypeScript doesn't enforce this at runtime. If I make it `async`, the actual runtime value would be a Promise, not a string. That would break tests.\n\nHmm, so the issue is that the `emit` method is async and the handlers run inside the `emit` call. Since `emit` must be called without `await`, the handlers run asynchronously after the function returns.\n\nIn a real application, this would be fine because events would eventually be handled. But in tests, we need to wait for the handlers to complete.\n\nOptions:\n1. In tests, I could use `setTimeout` or `await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 0))` to let the async handlers run\n2. I could have the services await the emit calls - but that would make the methods return promises\n\nActually, wait. Let me think about this more carefully. The `emit` method is:\n```ts\nasync emit(event: string, payload: unknown): Promise<void> {\n    this.eventLog.push({ event, payload, timestamp: Date.now() });\n    const handlers = this.handlers.get(event);\n    if (handlers) {\n      for (const handler of handlers) {\n        await handler(payload);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n```\n\nThe `await` inside the `for` loop means each handler is awaited. If the emit is called without await (like in `catalog.createProduct()`), the promise is returned but not awaited. The handlers will run on the microtask queue.\n\nHowever, in vitest (which uses fake timers by default?), the handlers may never run synchronously.\n\nWait, actually vitest doesn't use fake timers by default. The issue is that `emit` is async, and without `await` on the caller side, the handlers run asynchronously.\n\nLet me check if I should make the event-emitting methods async. Looking at the interfaces:\n\n```ts\ncreateProduct(name: string, price: number, category: string): string;\n```\n\nIf I make it `async`, it returns `Promise<string>`. TypeScript should complain about this. Let me check if TypeScript allows this...\n\nActually, TypeScript might not complain because of the way structural typing works. Let me test this. If the interface expects `() => string` and I provide `async () => string`, the return type would be `Promise<string>`. TypeScript should flag this as an error.\n\nBut wait, in the test, the test calls `catalog.createProduct(...)` synchronously and gets back a string. If I make it async, it would return a Promise, and the test would break.\n\nThe real issue is that the `EventBus.emit` is async but the service methods that call it are synchronous. The handlers don't get a chance to run until the next microtask.\n\nLet me think about the best approach:\n\n1. **Make the bus.emit synchronous**: But we can't modify bus.ts!\n2. **Make service methods async**: But the interfaces specify synchronous returns.\n3. **Use `queueMicrotask` or `setTimeout`**: Not practical.\n4. **Adjust the test to wait for async handlers**: Use `await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve))` after each event-emitting call.\n\nOption 4 seems most practical for tests. But the services also need to actually work correctly when events are awaited. Let me look at which tests are failing:\n\n1. Handler-based tests (events array) - handlers run async\n2. Analytics tests (event counts, revenue, top products) - analytics handlers run async\n\nThe CRUD tests all pass - they don't depend on events being handled synchronously.\n\nWait, actually I need to reconsider. Looking at the analytics test:\n```ts\nsys.cart.addItem(cid, pid, 2);\n```\nThis calls `bus.emit(Events.CART_UPDATED, payload)` without await. The analytics handler that updates `productOrderCounts` runs asynchronously. So by the time we call `analytics.getTopProducts(2)`, the handler may not have run yet.\n\nAnd the revenue test:\n```ts\nawait sys.payment.processPayment(oid, calc.total);\n```\nThis await the payment processing, which internally calls `bus.emit(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, payload)`. But the analytics handler for PAYMENT_PROCESSED runs inside the `emit` call, which is awaited by `processPayment`. So the handler should have run...\n\nWait, let me re-read the payment service:\n```ts\nasync processPayment(orderId: string, _amount: number) {\n  // ...\n  const payload: PaymentProcessedEvent = { orderId, status, transactionId, error };\n  bus.emit(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, payload);\n  return { status, transactionId, error };\n}\n```\n\nThe `bus.emit` call is NOT awaited in the payment service either! So the payment handler runs async too.\n\nSo the issue is consistent: none of the event-emitting calls are awaited in any service.\n\nI need to make all event-emitting calls awaited. But the service interfaces return synchronous types (string, void, boolean, etc.), not promises.\n\nWait, TypeScript does allow this! If the interface says:\n```ts\ncreateProduct(name: string, price: number, category: string): string;\n```\n\nAnd I implement it as:\n```ts\nasync createProduct(name: string, price: number, category: string): Promise<string> { ... }\n```\n\nTypeScript should give an error because `Promise<string>` is not a